                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Structure and run the negotiation`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The meeting is live and the CEO will intervene once. Your first move decides everything: argue <strong>positions</strong> and you trade the same nine engineers back and forth; move to <strong>interests</strong> — Engineering needs stability and credit, you need the booking — and the integrative option appears. On the table: <strong>phased delivery</strong> (data-layer hardening AND a scoped module behind a feature flag, same quarter, with a binding next-quarter commitment). Commit enough engineers to de-risk the module (≈3–5), and when the CEO pushes for a quick call, decide whether <em>holding the process two more minutes beats a fast deal you re-open next gate</em>.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Lock the deal and protect the relationship`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Convert the verbal agreement into something durable and read it out to the CEO. A handshake on “we'll collaborate” is not an agreement — by 9 a.m. tomorrow both teams ask <strong>who gets the nine engineers</strong> and <strong>what do we tell the ₹88 crore customer</strong>. Lock the customer message and document the allocation. Then the part most teams skip: two people felt they “lost.” Give the at-risk PM and engineer a way to stay with dignity — <em>two senior departures at ₹45 lakh each, plus lost context, can cost more than the feature was worth</em>. Read out all four KPIs: agreement quality, relationship strength, innovation outcome, resolution time.`,

                    r1_interest_label:      `What is the Head of Engineering's real INTEREST behind the position “re-architect the data layer first”?`,
                    r1_own_label:           `And your own underlying interest behind “ship the AI module”?`,
                    r1_dial_label:          `Where is your confidence dial currently set as you walk in? (0 = deferential, 100 = dominant)`,
                    r1_dial_readout:        `Displayed confidence:`,
                    r1_dial_hint:           `The confidence paradox is an inverted-U: too little forfeits your legitimate, customer-backed case; too much hardens Engineering and silences the joint problem-solving innovation needs. The inflection point sits around 60.`,
                    int_stability:          `Stability and not being blamed for the next outage — the SLA-credit hit landed on Engineering, not Product`,
                    int_power:              `Pure territory — they want to control the roadmap and deny Product a win`,
                    int_budget:             `More headcount — the nine engineers are really a staffing grab, nothing technical`,
                    int_position:           `It is exactly what they say — they want the re-architecture for its own sake, full stop`,
                    own_booking:            `The ₹22 crore expansion booking and keeping the ₹88 crore anchor from going to renewal evaluation`,
                    own_ego:                `Winning — Product has lost the last two gates and I need this one`,
                    own_feature:            `The module itself — shipping this specific feature is the goal in and of itself`,

                    r2_mode_label:          `Which Thomas-Kilmann conflict mode do you LEAD with?`,
                    r2_fallback_label:      `Your FALLBACK mode if the lead mode stalls?`,
                    r2_recalibrate_label:   `Re-calibrate your displayed confidence for the meeting itself.`,
                    r2_recalibrate_readout: `Confidence to display:`,
                    r2_opening_label:       `Opening move if the counterpart opens combative?`,
                    mode_competing:         `Competing — assertive and uncooperative; press Product's case hard and win the room`,
                    mode_collaborating:     `Collaborating — assertive AND cooperative; surface both interests and search for an integrative option`,
                    mode_compromising:      `Compromising — split the difference; trade some engineers for a partial module scope`,
                    mode_avoiding:          `Avoiding — defer the hard call; ask the CEO for more time to study it`,
                    mode_accommodating:     `Accommodating — yield; let Engineering set the roadmap to keep the peace`,
                    fb_collaborating:       `Switch to collaborating — re-open the search for a joint option`,
                    fb_compromising:        `Switch to compromising — find a workable middle to close in the time left`,
                    fb_competing:           `Escalate to competing — push harder until they yield`,
                    fb_none:                `No fallback — hold the one mode whatever happens`,
                    open_match:             `Match their force — meet the combative open with equal firmness so I don't look weak`,
                    open_acknowledge:       `Acknowledge then redirect — name the shared stake (the anchor customer) and invite the real interest into the room`,
                    open_retreat:           `Retreat — go quiet and let them run, hoping the CEO steps in`,

                    r3_move_label:          `As the live negotiation opens, what is your first structural move?`,
                    r3_option_label:        `The integrative option you put on the table.`,
                    r3_engineers_label:     `Engineers (of 9) you commit to the data-layer hardening this quarter.`,
                    r3_engineers_hint:      `Phased delivery needs enough engineers on hardening to de-risk the module (≈3–5 is the integrative band); starving it ships an unstable module, over-loading it leaves nothing for the scoped feature.`,
                    r3_pressure_label:      `The CEO intervenes once, pushing for a quick decision. You…`,
                    move_interests:         `Move to interests — surface that Engineering needs stability/credit and you need the booking, then look for the integrative zone`,
                    move_positions:         `Argue positions — “our module vs. your re-architecture”, and let the better case win`,
                    move_split:             `Split the engineers — give each side a share of the nine and call it fair`,
                    opt_phased:             `Phased delivery — data-layer hardening AND a scoped module behind a feature flag, same quarter, with a binding next-quarter commitment`,
                    opt_moduleonly:         `Module only — ship the full AI module now, re-architecture slips to “later”`,
                    opt_rearchonly:         `Re-architecture only — give Engineering the quarter, the module slips two quarters`,
                    opt_vague:              `No firm option — agree to “collaborate more closely” and sort specifics afterwards`,
                    pres_hold:              `Hold the process — “two minutes to lock the integrative option properly beats a fast deal we re-open next gate”`,
                    pres_cave:              `Cave to speed — take whatever closes fastest to look decisive`,
                    pres_escalate:          `Ask the CEO to decide — hand the call up rather than finish it`,

                    r4_customer_label:      `What do you tell the two anchor customers (₹149 crore combined)?`,
                    r4_allocation_label:    `Is the 9-engineer allocation locked and documented by 9 a.m. tomorrow?`,
                    r4_repair_label:        `How do the two heads signal a restored relationship so the at-risk PM and engineer stay?`,
                    r4_finalconf_label:     `Final confidence calibration as you read the deal out to the CEO. (0–100)`,
                    r4_finalconf_readout:   `Confidence at the read-out:`,
                    cust_specific:          `A specific message per customer — what ships this quarter (scoped module, flagged) and what is committed next quarter, in writing`,
                    cust_generic:           `A generic “exciting roadmap ahead” note to both — no dates, no specifics`,
                    cust_none:              `Nothing yet — tell them once the build is further along`,
                    alloc_documented:       `Yes — named owners, the split, and the next-quarter commitment written down and shared with both teams`,
                    alloc_verbal:           `Verbal only — we shook hands; the details will sort themselves out`,
                    alloc_open:             `Still open — we'll finalise allocation next week`,
                    rep_joint:              `A joint announcement — both heads co-present the deal as a shared win and give the at-risk PM and engineer visible ownership of the phased plan`,
                    rep_private:            `A quiet word — you'll each reassure your own person privately, separately`,
                    rep_ignore:             `Nothing — the deal is sound on paper; people will get over it`,

                    warn_dial_range:        `The confidence dial must be a value between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_recalibrate_range: `The confidence calibration must be between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_engineers_range:   `You can only allocate between 0 and 9 of the shared engineers.`,
                    warn_finalconf_range:   `The final confidence calibration must be between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_stability:     `<strong>That is the interest, not the position.</strong> Engineering doesn't want the re-architecture for its own sake — they want stability and not to be blamed for the next outage after the ₹3 crore SLA hit landed on them. Name that, and a phased option that protects them while you ship becomes visible.`,
                    coach_r1_position:      `<strong>You read the position as the interest.</strong> “Re-architect first” is the demand; the need underneath is stability and not carrying the next outage. Stop at the position and you'll spend the meeting trading the same nine engineers.`,
                    coach_r1_power:         `<strong>Assuming pure territory poisons the room before you sit down.</strong> If you walk in believing Engineering only wants to deny you a win, you'll negotiate against a caricature — and miss the real, solvable interest in stability.`,
                    coach_r1_budget:        `<strong>This isn't a headcount grab.</strong> Reducing Engineering's case to “they just want more people” misreads the technical-debt risk that drove the last outage. The interest is stability, and it is legitimate.`,
                    coach_r1_ego:           `<strong>“I need to win this one” is the most expensive interest in the room.</strong> The last two gates are sunk cost. If your real interest is ego rather than the booking, you'll over-confidence your way into a deal that costs you the engineer.`,
                    coach_r1_overconfident: `<strong>You walked in at near-maximal confidence.</strong> That asserts your case — and past the inflection point it hardens Engineering and shuts down the listening innovation needs. You may win the room and lose the engineer.`,
                    coach_r1_underconfident:`<strong>That dial is too low to defend a customer-backed case.</strong> Your AI module is what the ₹88 crore anchor asked for. Walk in this deferential and Engineering sets the whole roadmap while your legitimate interest goes unspoken.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named Engineering's real interest (stability) AND your own (the booking, not the ego), and set your confidence near the inflection point. That is exactly the calibration that keeps both voices in the room.`,
                    coach_r2_collaborating: `<strong>Collaborating is the mode the integrative zone needs.</strong> Assertive and cooperative keeps your case alive while searching for the joint option. Hold it with calibrated confidence and a deliberate fallback, and the CEO sees decisiveness, not indecision.`,
                    coach_r2_competing:     `<strong>Competing can win the room in ten minutes.</strong> But the Head of Engineering goes silent, your senior engineer updates her LinkedIn, and you've scored high on speed and low on relationship. Did you resolve the conflict or postpone it to the next exit interview?`,
                    coach_r2_compromising:  `<strong>Compromising closes — but splitting the difference is not the same as creating value.</strong> Trading some engineers for a partial scope leaves the integrative option on the table. Useful as a fallback; weak as the opening move.`,
                    coach_r2_avoiding:      `<strong>Avoiding burns the one thing you don't have: the meeting.</strong> Asking the CEO for more time when the constraint is one session reads as ducking the call — and the booking and the renewal don't wait.`,
                    coach_r2_accommodating: `<strong>You were so accommodating that Engineering set the entire roadmap.</strong> Your AI module slipped two quarters — and your anchor customer asked for that module. Where was the firmness your own case deserved?`,
                    coach_r2_nofallback:    `<strong>Leading with collaboration but holding no fallback is a risk.</strong> If the counterpart stonewalls, one mode held rigidly stalls. The discipline is switching deliberately — keep a fallback ready.`,
                    coach_r2_overconfident: `<strong>Even collaborating fails at maximal confidence.</strong> Past the inflection point your displayed confidence hardens the counterpart no matter what mode you announce. Bring the dial back toward 60.`,
                    coach_r2_matchforce:    `<strong>Matching force with force escalates a combative open.</strong> Meeting competing with competing wins the first exchange and loses the relationship. Acknowledge the shared stake first, then redirect to the real interest.`,
                    coach_r3_interests:     `<strong>Now you're negotiating the real problem.</strong> Surfacing that Engineering needs stability and you need the booking turns a zero-sum fight over nine engineers into a search for the integrative option. The phased deal lives here.`,
                    coach_r3_positions:     `<strong>You're trading the same nine engineers back and forth.</strong> Stop. What does Engineering actually need — stability, or credit, or not being blamed for the next outage? Find the interest and the integrative option appears.`,
                    coach_r3_split:         `<strong>Splitting the nine satisfies neither roadmap.</strong> A clean split feels fair but ships an under-resourced module AND an under-resourced re-architecture. Fair is not the same as integrative.`,
                    coach_r3_moduleonly:    `<strong>Shipping the module and slipping the re-architecture is winning the battle.</strong> It hits the booking — and leaves Engineering carrying the instability and the blame. That is exactly the “win” that costs you the engineer.`,
                    coach_r3_rearchonly:    `<strong>Giving Engineering the whole quarter forfeits your case.</strong> The module slips two quarters and the ₹88 crore anchor that asked for it starts evaluating a competitor. Your legitimate, customer-backed interest just went unspoken.`,
                    coach_r3_vague:         `<strong>You shook hands on “we'll collaborate.”</strong> Tomorrow morning both teams ask: who gets the nine engineers, and what do we tell the ₹88 crore customer? An agreement you can't operationalise by 9 a.m. is not an agreement. Lock the specifics.`,
                    coach_r3_starved:       `<strong>You starved the hardening.</strong> A scoped module shipped on an un-hardened data layer is the next outage — and another ₹3 crore in SLA credits. The phased option only works if you fund the de-risking.`,
                    coach_r3_overloaded:    `<strong>You put almost everyone on hardening.</strong> Engineering is happy, but nothing is left to build the scoped module — so nothing ships for the customer this quarter. The integrative band is ≈3–5, not all nine.`,
                    coach_r3_cave:          `<strong>You closed fast to look decisive.</strong> A shallow deal the teams resent reopens at the next gate. The CEO scores all four KPIs, not just the clock — was fast the same as resolved?`,
                    coach_r3_escalate:      `<strong>Handing the call up is the loss both teams feared.</strong> If the CEO decides unilaterally, both sides read it as defeat — and that is precisely when the senior PM and engineer walk. The whole point was that the two heads resolve it.`,
                    coach_r4_joint:         `<strong>That is relationship repair done right.</strong> Both heads co-presenting the deal as a shared win, with the at-risk PM and engineer given visible ownership, signals to both teams that no one lost. That is what keeps the ₹45 lakh talent.`,
                    coach_r4_ignore:        `<strong>Your deal is sound on paper, but two people felt they lost.</strong> Give them no way to stay with dignity and you may pay two ₹45 lakh departures plus lost context — more than the feature was worth. How do both heads signal that no one lost?`,
                    coach_r4_verbal:        `<strong>“We shook hands” is not an allocation.</strong> Without named owners and a written split, both teams will re-litigate the nine engineers by Monday. Document it tonight or the agreement evaporates.`,
                    coach_r4_open:          `<strong>Leaving allocation “open until next week” reopens the deadlock.</strong> The whole exercise was to resolve it in one meeting. An unallocated agreement is the deadlock with a handshake on top.`,
                    coach_r4_generic:       `<strong>An “exciting roadmap” note tells the ₹88 crore customer nothing.</strong> They asked for a specific capability and they're 90 days from renewal. Without dates and specifics, the generic note reads as a stall.`,
                    coach_r4_nocustomer:    `<strong>Telling the anchor customers nothing is the riskiest move.</strong> The ₹88 crore contract renews in 90 days and one of them has already hinted at a competitor. Silence now is how the renewal evaluation starts.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
