                    insight_integrative:    `Integrative resolution: you moved from positions to interests, calibrated your confidence near the inflection point, and structured a phased deal that ships a scoped module while hardening the data layer — an operationalisable agreement, an intact relationship, and a genuinely innovative outcome, all inside one meeting.`,
                    insight_won_lost:       `You won the room and lost the engineer. The agreement holds on paper, but over-confidence and a thin relationship triggered a senior departure — congratulations on the feature; the next exit interview is the real cost of that “win”.`,
                    insight_vague:          `Handshake, no agreement. You ended on “we'll collaborate” with no engineer allocation or customer message — by 9 a.m. tomorrow both teams reopen exactly the deadlock you were sent to resolve.`,
                    insight_short:          `You didn't fully resolve the deadlock. The agreement, the relationship and the innovative option still tell slightly different stories — enough that the next innovation gate reopens the same fight.`,
                    insight_attrition:      `Attrition triggered: a respected senior who felt they “lost” is leaving. At ~₹45 lakh per replacement plus lost context, a deal that hits the number while breaking the relationship can cost more than the feature was worth.`,
                    insight_positions:      `You never fully surfaced the interests. Negotiating positions — your module vs. their re-architecture — kept you trading the same nine engineers instead of finding the integrative zone hidden behind them.`,
                    insight_lowinnovation:  `Innovation outcome stayed low: you closed a deal but created little integrative value. A negotiation that wins by splitting the difference can hit the quarter while leaving the breakthrough — and the team's capacity for the next one — on the table.`,
                    insight_rushed:         `You resolved it fast — perhaps too fast. Optimising the clock to look decisive produced a shallow deal; resolution time is one KPI among four, not the objective.`,
                    insight_paradox:        `You held the confidence paradox: enough displayed confidence to protect a legitimate, customer-backed case, restrained enough to keep Engineering listening and the joint problem-solving alive. That inflection-point calibration is the whole skill.`,

                    hint_relationship:      `Relationship strength is collapsing — you're heading for the departure that costs ₹45 lakh and the next outage's context. Pull confidence back toward the inflection point and give the at-risk people a way to stay with dignity.`,
                    hint_vague:             `Your agreement isn't operationalisable. Lock the engineer allocation in writing and a specific customer message, or tomorrow's 9 a.m. reopens the deadlock.`,
                    hint_interests:         `You're still negotiating positions. Surface what Engineering actually needs — stability, credit, not carrying the next outage — and the integrative option appears.`,
                    hint_innovation:        `Innovation outcome is low. A split-the-difference deal hits the quarter but creates no integrative value — reach for the phased option that gives both sides their real interest.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — agreement, relationship and innovation all healthy. Lock the specifics and repair the relationship with a joint signal so the at-risk talent stays.`,

